The '''Crane''' is a technique used for landing a jump in [[Parkour]]. Cranes also serve as an accurate way to land onto of an obstacle from a running jump, [[precision]] , or [[vault]]. The landing of a crane involves the practitioner landing with his or her leg on top of the object, with the toe of the other foot perpendicularly touching the side of that object. There are some variations to the crane, though rather than personal taste, each variation is used for different speeds and environments.

{{Infobox technique
|image = 
|discipline = [[Freerunning]]
|difficulty = Advanced
}}
The '''Dash Bomb''' is a [[vault]] used in [[freerunning]] where the practitioner performs a [[front flip]] immediately out of a [[dash vault]] without their feet touching the ground in-between. It is considered to be one of the most difficult vaults to learn.

{{Infobox technique
|image = 6-73cc938fd7581f062127632b608d9fd5.jpg
|discipline = [[Parkour]]
|difficulty = Intermediate
}}
The '''Dash Vault''' is a [[vault]] used in [[Parkour]] in which a practitioner jumps over an obstacle feet first then leads the body away from the object with the hands. This vault is considered a more difficult vault to perform because of its difficulty to transfer momentum.

{{Infobox technique
|image = DK.jpg
|discipline = [[Parkour]]
|difficulty = Intermediate
}}
A '''Double Kong Vault''' is a [[vault]] used in [[parkour]]. It is similar to a regular [[Kong Vault|Kong]], but is used to get over two obstacles (or one long obstacle). The approach and takeoff is exactly like a regular kong, but rather than letting one's feet touch, the traceur pushes off twice.

{{Infobox technique
|image = 
|discipline = [[Freerunning]]
|difficulty = Intermediate
}}
A '''flyaway''', also sometimes simply called a '''swing gainer''', is a [[flip]] used in [[freerunning]] where the practitioner does a [[lache]] bar swing directly into a [[gainer]] backflip.

{{Infobox technique
|image = 
|discipline = [[Parkour]]
|difficulty = Basic
}}
The '''Jackie Chan Vault''' (or more commonly just the '''Chan Vault''') is a [[vault]] used in [[parkour]] where the practitioner performs a no-handed [[dash vault]]. It is a technique that mimics the flying kicks of Jackie Chan. Because of this, it is one of the only vaults where no contact is made with the obstacle at any point during the movement.

{{Infobox technique
|image = 
|discipline = [[Parkour]]
|difficulty = Intermediate
}}
The '''Kash Vault''' is a [[vault]] used in [[parkour]] consisting of a combination of the [[kong vault]] and [[dash vault]] where the practitioner begins with a kong vault, but during the middle of the vault swings their legs through their arms instead of going straight over. This vault is considered a relatively easy vault because of its slow speed, but for beginners this vault can be tricky.

{{Infobox technique
|image = Koala Climb.png
|discipline = [[Parkour]]
|difficulty = Intermediate
}}
'''Koala Climb''' is a climbing technique in [[Parkour]] used to easily scale pole-like obstacles.

{{Infobox technique
|image = Blog-parkour-kong.jpg
|discipline = [[Parkour]]
|difficulty = Intermediate
}}
The '''Kong Vault''' (also originally known as the '''Cat Pass''') is a [[vault]] used in [[parkour]] for moving over an object and gaining distance. It is an advanced variation of the [[monkey vault]]. Kongs are useful for moving over long objects, such as large barriers. The same movement can be used to move over rails and fences. Kong Vaults are commonly used for distance and power, instead of speed. Though they take extra time to learn, they are one of the most common and should be trained early on. There are a number of variations of the Kong Vault. Take note that when you use the Kong Vault, you have to dive towards the obstacle on which the Kong Vault will be performed, such as a picnic bench

{{Infobox technique
|image = 
|discipline = [[Freerunning]]
|difficulty = Advanced
}}
The '''Monkey Gainer''', or similarly the '''Kong Gainer''', is a [[vault]] used in [[freerunning]] which involves a practitioner performing a [[monkey vault]]/[[kong vault]] and using the leverage from their arms to push themselves immediately into a [[gainer]] without their feet coming into contact with the ground. Due to the high level of difficulty regarding this trick a distinction is generally not made over whether the practitioner uses a monkey vault (hands touch first) or a kong vault (jump before hands touch).

